      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To ensure your high-density racking stacks remain stable, you must treat the center of gravity like a tightrope walker trying to balance a stack of very heavy, very short, very uncooperative goblins. If you don't get the center of gravity right, the whole tower will tip over like a goblin trying to juggle three anvils while wearing a top hat.\n\nRegarding interlocking, absolutely, you should interlock the pallets. Think of it like a goblin trying to build a tower out of spaghetti; if you just stack them flat, it's a disaster waiting to happen. But if you weave them together, like a goblin trying to weave a basket out of his own tail hair, you create a structure that is far more resistant to the chaotic forces of gravity.\n\nHere is the best way to handle it:\n\n1.  **Center of Gravity**: Keep the heaviest items at the bottom, just like a goblin always puts his heaviest bag of gold coins at the bottom of his backpack so he doesn't fall over when he runs. The higher you go, the lighter the load should be. If you put a light goblin on top of a heavy goblin, the top g",
