    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the earlier slip-up; I was just trying to keep my thoughts organized, but it seems I got a bit too carried away with the goblin metaphors and accidentally started describing a goblin's backpack instead of a warehouse pallet! I can certainly provide the professional breakdown you need now, and I promise to keep the analogies strictly professional from here on out.\n\nRegarding the **base layer weight distribution**, the goal is to create a wide, stable footprint. The heaviest and most stable items should be placed on the bottom layer to lower the overall center of gravity. This prevents the entire stack from tipping forward or backward if a forklift bumps into the side. It's essentially about ensuring the base is as broad and heavy as possible to counteract any lateral forces.\n\nFor **interlocking patterns**, the most effective method is the **bricklaying pattern** (similar to how bricks are laid in masonry). In this pattern, the second layer is offset so that it sits directly over the gaps between the pallets in the layer below. This creates a continuous, interlocking structure that distributes weight evenly across the entire base and prevents individual pallets from shifting independently. This significantly increases stability compared to stacking pallets directly on",
